What:		/proc/ttl_ctl
Date:		October 2026
Contact:		Custom Android kernel maintainer
Description:
		Available with CONFIG_IP_FORWARD_TTL_PUBLIC_CONTROL=y. The kernel
		creates this node with mode 0666. Any user/application can read
		or change the opener's network namespace IPv4 forwarding TTL
		mode without root, capabilities, or a UID allowlist.

		Read returns "0\n" (normal decrement, default) or "1\n" (preserve).
		Write one of "0" or "1", optionally surrounded by whitespace.
		The maximum write size is 32 bytes. Embedded NULs, multiple
		values/commands, and other numeric values are rejected without
		changing the setting. Zero-length writes do nothing. Successful
		writes return their byte count; invalid input returns EINVAL,
		oversized input E2BIG, allocation failure ENOMEM, and bad user
		pointers EFAULT.

		This is an alias for net.ipv4.ip_forward_ttl_mode, not a separate
		setting. Writes to either interface are visible through the other.
		The sysctl retains its existing permissions. If SYSCTL is disabled,
		the public proc interface still controls the same forwarding field.

		Each open file pins the opener's network namespace until close.
		Passing an open descriptor or calling setns() does not retarget
		it. New namespaces start in mode 0. Concurrent writes are atomic
		at the mode-field level; no exclusive controller is imposed.

		All existing forwarding paths and TTL expiry checks are retained.
		Ordinary forwarding still rejects TTL <= 1 in either mode. IPv6
		Hop Limit, local output TTL, hardware/vendor paths, and BPF
		forwarding that bypass these software hooks are not controlled.

		SELinux exemptions use this node's private proc inode marker,
		shared with the dedicated public-control hook dispatch. They
		permit read/write, getattr and zero-length O_TRUNC handling for
		this inode only. They do not disable enforcing mode, grant root,
		permit execute/owner/mode changes, or exempt arbitrary proc or
		sysctl files. Read-only/hidden mounts and other LSMs still apply.
